"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[5406],{95406:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"03-risk","title":"Lesson 03. Anatomy of Market Risk: Testing Personal Drawdown Tolerance","sourceFingerprint":"4fb0d546e7c64162858da0b32b66b33b802abbd623220564bc8958965b82d4a4","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"**Chapter 3 \xb7 Resilience Practicum \xb7 Estimated reading time: 6 minutes**"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"# Anatomy of Market Risk: Testing Your Personal Drawdown Tolerance"},{"id":"b003","markdown":"> \\"Investment risk is not measured by complex mathematical formulas from academic textbooks, but by an elevated pulse at the moment when your brokerage balance rapidly turns crimson red.\\""},{"id":"b004","markdown":"## What Lies Behind the Word \\"Risk\\""},{"id":"b005","markdown":"In academia, people love to describe market uncertainty using Greek letters and volatility charts. However, for a private investor, the harsh reality looks far more mundane: you log into your mobile terminal and discover that instead of the deposited **500,000 ₽**, the screen displays only **330,000 ₽**."},{"id":"b006","markdown":"At that moment, only one question echoes in your mind: *\\"What should I do right now?\\"*"},{"id":"b007","markdown":"Remember the fundamental rule of a long-term investor: **a paper loss in value becomes an irreversible financial loss only when you succumb to panic and hit the sell button.**"},{"id":"b008","markdown":"## The Impartial Language of Facts: A Chronicle of Russian Drawdowns"},{"id":"b009","markdown":"To master composure, it is helpful to look at real historical precedents from the domestic stock market:"},{"id":"b010","markdown":"1. **The Crash of February 24, 2022.** During a single trading session, the estimated MOEX index collapsed by 33%. A portfolio owner holding **1,000,000 ₽** in equities at the morning open saw an evaluation of approximately **670,000 ₽** by the end of the day.\\n2. **The Five-Year Correction After the All-Time High.** In October 2021, the benchmark MOEX index recorded an all-time high around **4,290 points**. Five years later, in mid-October 2026, it closed at **2,426 points**—market capitalization contracted by 43% from the peak. Even factoring in the constant reinvestment of all dividends (the MCFTR total return index), the trajectory was tough: a decline from **8,126 points** (October 2021) to **7,003 points** (October 2026), representing a −14% drawdown over a five-year stretch for those who entered at the very crest of the wave.\\n3. **The Illusion of Absolute Bond Stability.** Sovereign debt obligations are also subject to price storms when the CBR tightens monetary policy. The RGBI government bond index lost over 25% of its value during aggressive key rate hike cycles. In October 2024, the indicator dropped below the psychological threshold of **100 points** for the first time since spring 2022. Investors holding \\"long-term\\" fixed-coupon OFZ bonds who were forced to sell at that moment suffered a painful loss, despite the top-tier creditworthiness of the issuer—the Ministry of Finance of the Russian Federation."},{"id":"b011","markdown":"None of these crises wiped out the capital of those who properly distributed their savings across different baskets and demonstrated patience. But each became a financial catastrophe for those who put everything into a single instrument and capitulated at the very bottom."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"## Four Scenarios Where Money Is Lost"},{"id":"b013","markdown":"Most beginner investors associate risk exclusively with total collapse. However, in real life, the loss of capital almost always takes one of four forms:"},{"id":"b014","markdown":"| Threat Type | How It Happens | How to Protect Yourself |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Temporary Drawdown** | Asset quotes decline due to the news background or a shift in the economic cycle. | Recognize the temporary nature of the drawdown and continue following the original strategy. |\\n| **Forced Liquidation** | The market has crashed, and a family emergency arises (urgent repairs, medical care). You have to close positions at an actual loss. | Maintain an untouchable reserve (emergency fund) in high-yield savings accounts or liquidity funds (LQDT). |\\n| **Psychological Breakdown** | There is enough spare cash, but the fear of declining prices paralyzes the mind. An individual sells assets hoping to \\"save what remains,\\" only for the market to reverse upward. | Adopt a conservative asset allocation and reduce the frequency of checking your brokerage account. |\\n| **Fatal Selection Error** | Buying shares of a single dubious issuer, the collapse of a pyramid scheme, or a friend\'s questionable startup. The money is burned irreversibly. | Broad diversification through funds and avoiding investments in non-transparent ventures. |"},{"id":"b015","markdown":"## Express Test: The Limit of Your Psychological Resilience"},{"id":"b016","markdown":"Answer this question honestly, imagining that you have **100,000 ₽** placed in a brokerage account, and six months later you see the following figures:"},{"id":"b017","markdown":"* **Balance: 90,000 ₽ (−10%).** If such a decline deprives you of sleep and triggers overwhelming anxiety, your profile is strictly conservative. The core of your portfolio should consist of money market instruments, short OFZ bonds, and bank deposits.\\n* **Balance: 75,000 ₽ (−25%).** You feel disappointed, yet maintain self-control and refrain from panic selling. A moderate share of broadly diversified equities is accessible to you.\\n* **Balance: 60,000 ₽ (−40%).** You calmly view the drawdown as a seasonal sale on quality assets and are ready to buy more securities at discounted prices. Your portfolio can include a significant allocation to equity instruments."},{"id":"b018","markdown":"> **MakeCash Rule:** Almost all novice investors drastically overestimate their courage until they face an actual portfolio decline with their own hard-earned money. Begin your journey with a minimal allocation to risky assets. You will always have time to increase your risk exposure."},{"id":"b019","markdown":"## The Investment Horizon Determines the Outcome"},{"id":"b020","markdown":"The severity of a drawdown depends entirely on the timeframe in which you will need your savings:"},{"id":"b021","markdown":"* **Money will be needed in 12 months (mortgage down payment):** a 30% drawdown in stocks is a disaster. Over a year, the market may not only fail to recover, but drop even deeper.\\n* **Capital is formed for a 15–20 year horizon:** a deep drawdown is merely an ordinary pothole on a long journey. It doesn\'t prevent you from reaching your final destination, as long as you don\'t turn the car around in a fit of panic."},{"id":"b022","markdown":"**The Golden Rule of Urgency:** The closer the date of the targeted expense, the smaller the share of floating-price assets that should be at your disposal. Capital with a horizon of up to 3 years is parked exclusively in the protected segment."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"## Complementary Effect: The Seasonal Goods Principle"},{"id":"b024","markdown":"For effective portfolio protection, assets must be tied to fundamentally different factors of the economic environment."},{"id":"b025","markdown":"Think of a street vendor who puts both cold drinks and raincoats on display. On a sweltering afternoon, drinks sell out, and in a downpour, raincoats do. The stall\'s total revenue remains stable day in and day out. Unlike his neighbor who sells only lemonade, he earns less on the hottest day of the year, but avoids facing bankruptcy during a prolonged autumn."},{"id":"b026","markdown":"With money, the logic is identical:\\n* When stock prices fall, reliable OFZ generate guaranteed coupon income.\\n* When the national currency depreciates, gold (for example, via the exchange-traded instrument #PAXG or bullion) and foreign currency assets (pegged to a basket of yuan or settlement instruments in USDT) show growth in ruble terms."},{"id":"b027","markdown":"> ⚠️ Buying shares of 10 domestic oil giants is not risk distribution. If commodity prices fall or industry taxation changes, they will decline synchronously. True diversification only works across fundamentally different asset classes."},{"id":"b028","markdown":"## The Practice of Annual Rebalancing"},{"id":"b029","markdown":"Market fluctuations continually disrupt your portfolio\'s balance. If stocks grow 1.5 times over a year, their percentage share will exceed the target, leaving your entire capital excessively vulnerable."},{"id":"b030","markdown":"Once every 12 months, restore your asset allocation to its baseline targets:\\n1. Partially lock in profits on the asset class that showed outperforming growth.\\n2. Allocate the freed-up funds to buy those assets that temporarily lagged behind or fell in price."},{"id":"b031","markdown":"This strict algorithm automatically forces you to follow the commandment of smart investing: sell what is expensive and buy more of what has become cheap, ignoring emotional noise."},{"id":"b032","markdown":"## \uD83E\uDDEE Calculation in the Margins of a Notebook: Anatomy of Behavior During an Account Crash"},{"id":"b033","markdown":"Let\'s examine what different investor decisions lead to when a portfolio drops from **1 000 000 ₽** to **670 000 ₽** (drawdown −33%):"},{"id":"b034","markdown":"```\\nInitial amount: 1 000 000 ₽\\nIndex drawdown: -33%\\nCurrent portfolio valuation: 670 000 ₽\\n```"},{"id":"b035","markdown":"* **Scenario A (Emotional Dump):**\\n  The investor closes all positions on the day of the bottom, locking in a loss of **330 000 ₽**. The account is left with 670 000 ₽. When the market returns to its previous levels over the next 1.5 years (+50% from the bottom), the investor\'s capital does not grow. The loss has become permanent.\\n* **Scenario B (Passive Hold):**\\n  The investor leaves the securities untouched and goes on living normal life. The market recovers by +50% from the trough:\\n  $$670\\\\,000 \\\\times 1{,}50 = 1\\\\,005\\\\,000\\\\text{ ₽}$$\\n  The balance fully recovers without additional investment thanks to the time factor.\\n* **Scenario C (Rebalancing and Disciplined Adding):**\\n  The investor had a defensive allocation in a liquidity fund (LQDT). During the drawdown, he takes 150 000 ₽ from the defensive portion and buys cheaper assets. The average entry price drops. When the market recovers by 50%, the capital doesn\'t just recover the loss, but enters net profit long before the index returns to historical highs."}],"terms":[{"id":"prosadka","term":"Drawdown","definition":"a decline in portfolio value from a historical peak to a trough."},{"id":"volatilnost","term":"Volatility","definition":"a measure of an asset\'s price variability over a specific period."},{"id":"rebalansirovka","term":"Rebalancing","definition":"restoring the original target asset allocation proportions in a portfolio."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"q1","q":"You need to pay for your child\'s college tuition in exactly 10 months. Where is the safest and most reasonable place to park the prepared funds?","options":["In gold bullion and tokenized gold (#PAXG), since precious metals historically outpace inflation.","In a large-cap stock index, as it could surge over six months driven by dividend season.","In reliable short-term instruments: a traditional bank deposit, money market funds (LQDT), or short OFZ bonds with a fixed coupon."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Over a horizon of less than 1–3 years, the stock market is unpredictable. If even a temporary 20–30% correction occurs, you would be forced to realize a loss to pay the bill. Short-term money should only be kept in instruments with guaranteed liquidity and a fixed value."},{"id":"q2","q":"What does a sound annual rebalancing procedure entail?","options":["Fully closing all positions in the brokerage account and withdrawing cash once a year.","Selling a certain portion of appreciated assets and using the proceeds to purchase lagging or depreciated instruments to restore the portfolio\'s original proportions.","Daily purchases of shares in companies that experienced the steepest price declines at the end of the trading session."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Rebalancing restores the target risk level. It automatically compels the investor to sell overheated parts of the portfolio and buy dipped, quality instruments, removing human emotion from the capital management process."},{"id":"q3","q":"Why is purchasing 10 different stock issues exclusively from Russian metallurgical plants not considered full diversification?","options":["Because the broker charges an increased commission for holding assets from a single industry.","Because these companies depend on the same factors: metal prices, export duties, the ruble exchange rate, and the overall trajectory of domestic demand.","Because the shares of any industrial plants never show growth."],"correct":1,"explanation":"True capital protection occurs when assets respond differently to the same macroeconomic events. Securities of companies within the same industry typically rise and fall together under the influence of shared commodity and regulatory factors."}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);